{"name":"Paisley","hex":"#726f7e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Paisley","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A town, the administrative centre of Renfrewshire council area, Scotland .","A locality in the Murray Mallee region, South Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Paisley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paisley (design)","blocks":[{"text":"Paisley or paisley pattern is an ornamental textile design using the boteh or buta, a teardrop-shaped motif with a curved upper end. Of Iranian/Persian origin, paisley designs became popular in the West in the 18th and 19th centuries, following imports of post-Mughal Empire versions of the design from India, especially in the form of Kashmir shawls, and were then replicated locally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Shawl_LACMA_M.84.244.1.jpg/330px-Shawl_LACMA_M.84.244.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paisley_(design)","wikidata":"Q937704"},{"title":"Paisley (name)","blocks":[{"text":"Paisley is an English and Scottish surname and a given name derived from the surname. The name is rising in popularity in English speaking countries such as the United States, where it has ranked among the top 1,000 names for newborn girls since 2006 and has ranked among the top 50 names for girls in recent years. It is a name that is notably more commonly used by whites in rural states in the United States than in urban areas. Spelling variants are also well-used."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paisley_(name)","wikidata":"Q65119447"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Persian_Silk_Brocade_-_Paisley_-_Persian_Paisley_-_Seyyed_Hossein_Mozhgani_-_1963.jpg/500px-Persian_Silk_Brocade_-_Paisley_-_Persian_Paisley_-_Seyyed_Hossein_Mozhgani_-_1963.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https: